# GN4 Search: proxy -> native migration

## Tags

* Assigned: alexm
* Keywords: GN4, search, xapian, tantivy, parity, proxy, native
* Type: Enhancement
* Status: In Progress



## Description

Search is the pilot for the proxy -> native migration pattern: gn4
first fronts gn3's xapian-backed /api/search behind its own envelope
and contract, then replaces the backend with a native engine inside
gn4. This note records the decisions, the state, and the engine
analysis that drives the next step.

## The migration pattern

```
user / LLM
  |
gn4  GET /search   (envelope, tokens, error contract -- FROZEN)
  |
  +-- phase A [MERGED]: forward q verbatim to gn3 /api3/search,
  |     rewrap hits; recorded gn3 responses double as test oracle
  |
  +-- phase B [IN REVIEW]: Engine seam -- routes delegate to an
  |     Engine trait; the gn3 forwarder is one impl; a native
  |     engine drops in without touching routes or contract
  |
  +-- phase C [NEXT]: native engine matches the recorded oracle at
        the parity floor; the gn3 forwarding path is deleted
```

## Decisions

1. [2026-10-04, Pjotr] GN4 is a new beast: port relevant code into
   gn4; duplication accepted; gn3 is dropped in time.
2. [2026-10-04, Pjotr] Search itself goes Rust; indexing stays as-is
   for now (built by the existing indexer -- his study decides its
   future).
3. [2026-10-04, Alex; accepted by Pjotr] Bridge: the proxy's recorded
   gn3 responses remain the test oracle; the native engine must match
   the /search contract; forwarding dies at parity.
4. [2026-10-05, Pjotr] Runtime dependencies settle on gn3 + gn-auth +
   mariadb, gn3 for validation only; no gn2, no gn-guile.
5. [2026-10-06, Alex] Parity is a FLOOR, not a target: match-set
   equality keyed (dataset, name) plus top-10 Jaccard >= 0.8. Exact
   cross-engine ranking is unattainable and not attempted.

## State (2026-10-06)

* Phase A merged to main: /search behind the envelope with an
  authorization token, hermetic offline tests (stub JWKS + locally
  signed tokens), and a Python contract mirror.
* Phase B on branch native-search-seam (pushed, awaiting review):
  src/search/ module with the Engine trait; routes unchanged in
  behavior; the module docs record the full xapian query grammar
  (every prefix, operator, range rule -- reconstructed from source
  with file:line provenance) as the requirement any native engine
  must satisfy.
* Corpus: 54 query shapes recorded from production /api3/search with
  an idempotent, re-runnable script (md5-identical across runs);
  error classes and empty-set shapes logged per shape.

## Engine analysis [2026-10-06]

A tantivy spike indexed a 609-document slice from the recorded
responses and ran the grammar through it. Findings:

* The recorded payload is a PROJECTION of the searchable document:
  gn3 indexes publication metadata and trait synonyms the API does
  not return ("alcohol": 1,197 hits; only 53 payloads contain the
  term). No native engine can reproduce gn3's match set from API
  responses -- the indexer's document construction is the real port,
  and it is engine-independent.
* Enrichment fields (mean, additive, lrs, geno_chr, geno_mb) are
  DB-filled at query time in gn3; a native engine ports those queries
  regardless of engine choice.
* tantivy: every sampled grammar construct is expressible (some via
  a small translator; NEAR approximate); ranges/keywords recall 1.000
  on the slice; cost is a 142-package Cargo.lock, not pure-Rust
  (zstd-sys), against a very active upstream.
* xapian-FFI: reuses the existing index, grammar, and the indexer's
  document construction; but the Rust binding crates are dormant
  (xapian 0.1.5, 2023; xapian-rs 0.3.0, 2024), so we would own a
  small bindgen layer over open-db/parser/mset; the C++ dependency
  stays in the guix build; the indexer question is deferred, not
  answered.
* Read: xapian-FFI fits the parity goal -- same engine makes the
  floor nearly automatic, and the indexer keeps running as-is.
  tantivy is right only if the goal shifts to quality metrics beyond
  xapian parity (Johannes's track), accepting the indexer rebuild
  plus the 142-package tree.

## Next step

A small binding spike against the real index: open the production
xapian database from Rust, configure the parser per the grammar
spec, run one query end-to-end, and prove guix can build and link
it. Disposable, days not weeks; it answers the two open risks
(the unsafe layer, the packaging) before committing to the port.

## One error contract for malformed input (agreed -- thumbs-up on the proposal, 2026-10-06)

Replace production's three accidental behaviors (500 ValueError,
500 InvalidOperationError, silent 200s) with one rule: all malformed
query input returns 422 invalid_query with a structured, typed body:

``` {"error": {"type": "invalid_query",
          "message": "Syntax: <expression> AND <expression>",
          "param": "query",
          "detail": {"xapian_error_type": "QueryParserError"}}} ```

Rationale: one machine-checkable rule instead of inherited crashes;
typed errors let LLM/API clients self-correct and retry (serves the
AI-discoverability track); every link is already built
(parse_query_checked -> ParseError -> structured 422). The parity
harness enforces whichever contract is chosen; Q42 re-baselines once
blessed, the && fixtures already record production's 200s.

## Open items

* Binding spike -- pending Pjotr's OK on the xapian-FFI direction.
* Authoritative behavior for invalid queries (updated 2026-10-06,
  supersedes the sanitize theory): libxapian 1.4.29 itself parses
  "a&&b", "alcohol &&", and "position:" -- production returns 200
  with hits (a&&b: 13,760, per the frozen corpus). Malformed
  processor VALUES are different: production crashes on them
  (position:abc -> 500 ValueError; Hs:foo:1M -> 500
  InvalidOperationError, curl-verified + corpus-fixture-recorded).
  Those 500s are implementation accidents, not design. See the
  proposed contract below.
* 14 corpus shapes return empty match sets at production: valid
  responses, but they weaken the parity floor; the query list may
  need re-shaping.
* Pjotr's indexer study (what builds the index today; document
  shape) -- feeds the native engine's document expectations.
* The indexer migration itself is a separate, later project once
  search is native.

